From 5db24348097517f27035fa826272527938cf0363 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bsp2020 <bsp2020@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 01:52:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] llama: batch the device reads of a context state save

A context checkpoint of a recurrent / hybrid model reads one sequence's
cells of every recurrent-state tensor, ~75 small ranges.  Under -sm
tensor each range is one synchronous read per device, so a checkpoint
paid ~150 device syncs and bounce copies (11.8 ms for 60 MiB on 2x
gfx1201, taken before every prompt chunk of a follow-up turn).

llama_io_write_host now queues every deferred read as
ggml_backend_tensor_get_async on the backend that owns the tensor, into
one pinned staging buffer kept by the context, synchronizes each backend
once and copies to the caller's buffer (5.5 ms).  States larger than
LLAMA_STATE_ASYNC_MAX_MIB (default 256) and LLAMA_STATE_ASYNC=0 keep the
synchronous path; so does any state with a tensor no context backend
owns.

The meta backend's get_tensor_async asserted offset == 0 and a single
segment, so it could not read a row range.  It now shares the
synchronous read's split arithmetic and queues the same per-device
reads on the simple backends.

Restores keep the synchronous path: batched they measured no faster.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
